course: medicine
lesson: 34
title: Exercise and posture
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Your body is built to move: running, climbing and jumping make the heart and lungs stronger, build muscle and bone, and even help you sleep, think and feel happier.
explain: When you run, your muscles need more oxygen, so you breathe faster and your heart pounds: that is your body doing its job, not a sign something is wrong.
explain: It doesn't have to be sport: a game of tag, a bike ride, a walk to school, a swim or dancing in the kitchen all count towards the hour a day children are advised to move.
explain: Bodies like change more than perfection: slouching for a while does no harm, but sitting in any one position for hours does, so get up, stretch and move often.
fact: UK guidance says children and young people should be active for an average of at least 60 minutes a day across the week.
fact: Activity makes the heart, lungs, muscles and bones stronger, and helps sleep, mood and concentration.
fact: Breathing faster during exercise is normal, because the muscles need more oxygen, and fitness builds up gradually.
fact: A heavy bag on one shoulder can strain the back and shoulders, so use both straps and pack only what you need; it rarely causes lasting damage.
fact: There is no single perfect posture; changing position often and getting up to move about every half hour is good for the body.
fact: A stitch is a sharp pain in the side during exercise, its exact cause is still not known for certain, it isn't dangerous, and slowing down and breathing deeply helps.
fact: A big meal or a lot of drink just before running makes a stitch more likely.
fact: A warm-up gets the blood flowing and prepares muscles and joints, but stretching alone doesn't prevent every injury.
fact: Muscle soreness a day or two after unusual exercise is normal as muscles adapt, and gentle movement helps.
fact: Swimming is great exercise and a lifesaving skill, and in England primary schools aim for every child to swim at least 25 metres; people can learn to swim at any age.
fact: Exercise releases chemicals in the brain that lift mood and reduce stress, so it isn't only about weight.
fact: Walking or cycling to school adds activity and is better for the air than a short car trip.
exercise: How long should children be active each day, on average? | At least 60 minutes | At least 10 minutes, if you run hard | Exactly 5 hours | Only on PE days, and never at weekends
exercise: Does activity have to be organised sport? | No, play and walking count too | Yes, only proper team sports with a coach count | Yes, only exercise done in a gym | Only running counts
exercise: Why do you get out of breath running? | Your muscles need more oxygen | Your lungs are too small for your body | It means you are unfit and probably ill | The air runs out
exercise: Is being out of breath during a race bad? | No, it's normal | Yes, stop exercising for good | Yes, it always means you have asthma | Only bad for girls
exercise: How should you carry a heavy school bag? | On both straps, packed light | On one shoulder, swapping each day | Dragged on the ground behind you | On your head
exercise: Is there one perfect posture? | No, changing position often is best | Yes, always sitting bolt upright, back straight | Yes, slouched down low in the chair | Yes, lying flat on the floor
exercise: How often should you get up from sitting? | About every half hour | Once a day, after lunch | Never during homework or it breaks your focus | Every five minutes exactly
exercise: Is a stitch dangerous? | No, slow down and breathe deeply | Yes, call 999 straight away | Yes, it's a small heart attack | Yes, it means you must stop sport for a month
exercise: What makes a stitch more likely? | A big meal just before running | Drinking a glass of water in the morning | Warming up properly before the start | Wearing trainers
exercise: Do scientists know exactly what causes a stitch? | Not for certain | Yes, it's a torn muscle in the side | Yes, it's a cracked rib | Yes, it's caused by bubbles in the blood
exercise: Why warm up before PE? | To get blood flowing and prepare muscles and joints | To make you tired so that you play more calmly in the game | To win the game | To cool the body down
exercise: Your legs ache the day after a long walk. What is it? | Normal soreness as muscles adapt | A tiny crack in one of the leg bones | A serious injury that needs a doctor | Growing pains always
exercise: How far does England aim for every child to swim at primary school? | At least 25 metres | 1 metre, a single stroke | 5 kilometres, out in the open sea | A full Channel crossing
exercise: How does exercise help mood? | It releases brain chemicals that lift mood and reduce stress | It makes you too tired to feel anything at all, good or bad | It only helps your weight, not your mind | It doesn't
exercise: Why is walking to school better than a short drive? | More activity and cleaner air | Cars are illegal near schools now | Walking is always faster than a car | It saves school dinners
test: "I'm puffing after 1 lap, I must be ill" — hmm? | No, faster breathing is normal; fitness builds up | Yes, see a doctor about it today | Yes, stop all sport until you feel better | It means your lungs are broken and need checking
test: Mate: "PE's the only exercise that counts". Right? | No, play, cycling and walking count too | Yes, only PE, everything else is just playing | Yes, and only football in PE | No, only gym counts
test: Teacher yells "sit up straight!" What's the bigger truth? | Moving and changing position often matters more than one posture | Slouching for even a minute can break your spine | Sitting still and upright for hours is perfect | Posture doesn't matter at all
test: Stitch halfway through sports day. Best move? | Slow down, breathe deeply, it'll pass | Sprint faster to get through it | Lie flat on the grass and call an ambulance | Drink a fizzy drink to settle it
test: What would happen if a child sat in front of screens all day and never moved? | Heart, bones and mood would likely suffer | Their bones would grow stronger from the rest | They'd sleep better | Nothing, the body doesn't need movement to stay healthy
test: Josh hauls all his books every day on one shoulder. What's sensible? | Use both straps and only pack what he needs | Carry even more books to build strength | Use the other shoulder only from now on | Nothing, bags can't hurt
test: Legs sore the day after a big hike. Should you stay in bed all week? | No, gentle movement helps sore muscles | Yes, they're injured and need complete rest | Yes, for a month to be safe | Rub them with ice only
test: In a cartoon a kid can't swim and is laughed at. What's true? | Swimming can be learned at any age, nothing to be ashamed of | Some people can never learn, however hard they try | Only fish can swim | It's shameful forever
test: You feel low after a bad day. What might help? | Going for a walk or a kick-about | Sitting alone in the dark to think it over | Skipping sleep to watch more videos | Eating only sweets
test: "stretching means I'll never get hurt" — true? | No, warm-ups help but don't prevent every injury | Yes, you'll never get hurt | Yes, if you stretch for a whole hour first | No, stretching causes injuries
test: Why might a big plate of pasta just before a race be a bad idea? | It makes a stitch more likely | It makes you run too fast and fall | Pasta has no energy | It cools your muscles down too much
exam: Pursuant to the guidance issued in the United Kingdom, determine the minimum average daily duration of physical activity recommended for children. | Sixty minutes | Ten minutes of brisk walking | Five hours, of which two are vigorous | Thirty seconds of vigorous activity
exam: Identify the principal reason for which respiration accelerates during exercise. | The muscles require an increased supply of oxygen | The lungs contract permanently with exertion | The heart ceases to pump during exertion | The air becomes thinner
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the so-called stitch experienced during running. | Its precise cause remains uncertain and it is not dangerous | It denotes a fracture of the lower ribs | It denotes a failure of the heart | It is invariably caused by dehydration
exam: Ascertain the most accurate characterisation of muscular soreness arising one or two days after unaccustomed exercise. | A normal consequence of the muscles adapting | A certain indication of serious injury | A symptom of an infectious disease of the muscles | Evidence of fractured bone
